Migrants",,United Kingdom,"The Partnership continues to collaborate with other UK food retailers to fund the Seasonal Workers Taskforce. The Taskforce involves scheme operators, growers, government departments and NGOs and aims to support improvements to the design of the seasonal workers scheme, safeguard workers’ rights and address risks of worker exploitation. In 2025 we have rolled out a worker survey on experiences and continued the expansion of the Just Good Work app to promote awareness of workers rights.",Migrant workers in fishing sector,Within your supply chains,Tier 2 suppliers,Migrants,,United Kingdom,"The Partnership has funded the development of the Just Good Work app into the fishing sector, which aims to educate workers on their rights in the UK and how to avoid recruitment scams. We are providing funding into 2026 for an outreach worker to build further awareness of the app among Filipino migrant fishers who make up a significant proportion of the UK workforce.",Gender discrimination in general merchandise sites,Within your supply chains,Tier 3 suppliers and below,Women,,Bangladesh,"In 2025 we carried out a detailed assessment of the role of female homeworkers within our woven basket supply chain in Bangladesh to ensure that workers well-being was protected within these decentralised production settings. Actions following have included developing a phased roadmap to enhance pay rates, upgrading ventilation in community workspaces and training community coordinators to provide ongoing monitoring of homeworking environments.","Abuse of vulnerability
Deception
Restriction of movement
Physical and sexual violence
Retention of identity documents
Debt bondage
Abusive working and living conditions
Excessive overtime",No,"Financial remediation, including repayment of recruitment fees
Change in policy
Change in training
Supporting victims via NGO
Supporting investigations by relevant authorities",No,"The focus of our human rights programmes is to improve working conditions throughout our operations and supply chains that provide goods and services to the Partnership. Our statement outlines how we measure the effectiveness of our due diligence programmes, including the number of non conformances closed on audit, spot checks conducted on supply sites and grievances raised. We have seen a year on year increase in whistleblowing cases. Targets for our work can be seen in our latest E&S report.",2026-07-29T08:01:35,https://downloads.modern-slavery-statement-registry.service.gov.uk/pdf-published/CszZr3X5/2026/JLP_Modern_Slavery_Statement_2026_FINAL.pdf
